GitHub does not say "this repository does not exist".

17
00:01:16,086 --> 00:01:20,955
For a repository you cannot see, it answers "authentication required",

18
00:01:20,955 --> 00:01:27,841
because a private repository with that name might exist and GitHub will not reveal it to strangers.

19
00:01:27,841 --> 00:01:33,197
So Git asks for a username (in this course's test setup prompts are disabled,

20
00:01:33,197 --> 00:01:41,057
so it stops with could not read Username; on your computer a login window or a Username for 'https://github.com':

21
00:01:41,057 --> 00:01:42,169
prompt appears).

22
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:45,733
Here is the fix.

23
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:48,180
Got it.

24
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:50,931
What do I need to remember?

25
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:59,416
GitHub = a Git server + collaboration features (PRs, issues, Actions, releases).

26
00:01:59,967 --> 00:02:04,761
All Git commands work with GitHub URLs exactly as with any remote.

27
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:11,049
A credential prompt for a "public" repository usually means a wrong name or no access.

28
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,970
I keep hearing about creating a GitHub repository.

29
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:18,555
What is it, really?

30
