00:07:35,867 --> 00:07:37,938
Let me try something.

105
00:07:37,938 --> 00:07:41,391
Use gh outside a repository folder:

106
00:07:41,933 --> 00:07:43,491
no git remotes found:

107
00:07:43,491 --> 00:07:48,091
gh picks the repository from the current folder's Git remotes.

108
00:07:48,091 --> 00:07:56,178
The lab folder is a Git repository, but without a GitHub remote, so gh cannot know which repository you mean.

109
00:07:56,178 --> 00:08:00,778
(Outside any repository the message is not a git repository.).

110
00:08:02,533 --> 00:08:07,878
Name it explicitly with -R OWNER/REPO, or cd into the clone:

111
00:08:09,633 --> 00:08:10,347
Got it.

112
00:08:10,347 --> 00:08:13,098
What do I need to remember?

113
00:08:13,633 --> 00:08:22,028
Code, branches and tags are Git; issues, PRs, Actions, releases and settings are GitHub.

114
00:08:22,567 --> 00:08:25,431
A clone contains only the Git part.

115
00:08:25,967 --> 00:08:32,326
gh is the command-line window to the GitHub part (-R OWNER/REPO outside a clone).

116
00:08:34,067 --> 00:08:38,241
What should I know about HTTPS authentication?

117
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:42,356
How Git proves who you are to GitHub over HTTPS:

118
00:08:42,356 --> 00:08:48,060
not with your account password (GitHub stopped accepting it for Git in 2021),

119
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:53,690
but with a token, handed to Git by a credential helper so you never type it.

120
00:08:55,433 --> 00:08:59,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

121
00:09:00,367 --> 00:09:04,407
Reading a public repository needs no credentials:

122
00:09:04,407 --> 00:09:05,478
Writing does.

123
00:09:05,478 --> 00:09:10,260
With no helper, Git has to ask (here prompts are disabled,

124
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:16,856
so it stops; on your computer you would see Username for 'https://github.com':):

125
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:18,542
Writing does.

126
00:09:18,542 --> 00:09:23,635
With no helper, Git has to ask (here prompts are disabled,

127
00:09:23,635 --> 00:09:30,659
so it stops; on your computer you would see Username for 'https://github.com':):

128
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:33,987
Let me try something.

129
00:09:33,987 --> 00:09:36,254
Do what many people try first:

130
00:09:36,254 --> 00:09:39,580
answer the prompt with the account password.

131
00:09:40,133 --> 00:09:42,367
Invalid username or token.

132
00:09:42,367 --> 00:09:47,607
Password authentication is not supported for Git operations.:

133
00:09:47,607 --> 00:09:50,871
GitHub accepts only tokens over HTTPS.

134
00:09:50,871 --> 00:09:53,448
Even a correct password fails.

135
00:09:55,200 --> 00:10:03,255
Log in once with the GitHub CLI (gh auth login, done in lesson 45) and connect it to Git:

136
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:05,713
Got it.

137
00:10:05,713 --> 00:10:08,464
What do I need to remember?

138
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:15,135
GitHub never accepts your account password for Git; HTTPS needs a token.

139
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:22,975
A credential helper (Git Credential Manager, gh auth setup-git) stores and supplies it.

140
00:10:23,533 --> 00:10:30,408
Prefer fine-grained, expiring, least-privilege tokens; in Actions use GITHUB_TOKEN.

141
00:10:32,167 --> 00:10:35,451
Can you show me SSH authentication?

142
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,699
How to authenticate to GitHub with an SSH key pair:

143
00:10:39,699 --> 00:10:43,762
create the key with ssh-keygen, trust GitHub's host key,

144
00:10:43,762 --> 00:10:49,130
add the public key to your account, and verify with ssh -T [email].

145
00:10:50,867 --> 00:10:55,266
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

146
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:56,009
1.

147
00:10:56,009 --> 00:10:57,889
Create a key pair.

148
00:10:58,433 --> 00:11:04,478
The .pub file is the part you share; id_ed25519 is the secret.

149
00:11:04,478 --> 00:11:04,673
2.

150
00:11:04,673 --> 00:11:07,013
Trust GitHub's host key.

151
00:11:08,767 --> 00:11:10,332
Let me try something.

152
00:11:10,332 --> 00:11:13,836
Test the connection before registering the key:

153
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:16,439
Permission denied (publickey):

154
00:11:16,439 --> 00:11:21,809
the network and GitHub's host key are fine (we reached GitHub and it answered),

155
00:11:21,809 --> 00:11:25,344
but no key ssh offered is registered on any account.

156
00:11:25,344 --> 00:11:28,335
The debug log shows which keys were offered:

157
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:34,209
Register the public key with your account (web:

158
00:11:34,209 --> 00:11:39,805
Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH key, paste the .pub line):

159
00:11:41,567 --> 00:11:42,280
Got it.

160
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:45,031
What do I need to remember?

161
00:11:45,567 --> 00:11:52,896
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 creates the pair; only the .pub part goes to GitHub.

162
00:11:53,433 --> 00:11:58,513
Verify GitHub's host key fingerprint once; known_hosts remembers it.

163
00:11:59,067 --> 00:12:05,736
ssh -T [email] tests; Permission denied (publickey) = no registered key was offered.

164
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:11,740
I keep hearing about SSH vs HTTPS.

165
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:14,110
What is it, really?

166
00:12:14,667 --> 00:12:17,706
Both protocols reach the same repository.

167
00:12:17,706 --> 00:12:25,637
We compare them in practice, switch a clone from one to the other, and fix the most common network problem:

168
00:12:25,637 --> 00:12:27,861
port 22 blocked by a firewall.

169
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:33,000
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

170
00:12:34,533 --> 00:12:36,811
Switch the remote to SSH:

171
00:12:36,811 --> 00:12:42,188
only the URL changes; branches, history and upstreams stay.

172
00:12:42,188 --> 00:12:49,933
Or let Git rewrite every GitHub HTTPS URL to SSH automatically, for all repositories:

173
00:12:50,467 --> 00:12:57,797
Or let Git rewrite every GitHub HTTPS URL to SSH automatically, for all repositories:

174
00:12:57,797 --> 00:13:04,696
git remote -v already shows the rewritten URL; the config file still says HTTPS.

175
00:13:06,433 --> 00:13:08,338
Let me try something.

176
00:13:08,338 --> 00:13:14,598
A network where outgoing port 22 is blocked (hotel, corporate proxy).

177
00:13:15,133 --> 00:13:21,119
Connection timed out / Connection refused comes before any authentication:

178
00:13:21,119 --> 00:13:29,209
it is a network problem, not a key problem (compare with Permission denied (publickey) in lesson 49,

179
00:13:29,209 --> 00:13:31,797
which means the network worked).

180
00:13:31,797 --> 00:13:36,813
GitHub offers SSH on port 443 too, at the host ssh.github.com:

181
00:13:38,567 --> 00:13:45,586
Make port 443 permanent for github.com, so the normal URLs keep working:

182
00:13:47,333 --> 00:13:48,047
Got it.

183
00:13:48,047 --> 00:13:50,798
What do I need to remember?

184
00:13:51,333 --> 00:13:56,460
Same repository, two transports:

185
00:13:56,460 --> 00:14:05,433
HTTPS (port 443, tokens) and SSH (port 22 or 443, keys).

186
00:14:05,967 --> 00:14:11,966
git remote set-url or url.<base>.insteadOf switch between them.

187
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:17,984
Timeouts = network; Permission denied (publickey) = authentication.
